\titledquestion{Binary Search Trees, Again}

\textbf{Binary Search Trees, Again}

Recall the type of integer \code{tree}s, from lecture:
\begin{codeblock}
  datatype tree = Empty | Node of tree * int * tree
\end{codeblock}

A \textit{binary search tree} is a value of type \code{tree}, that satisfies the
following definition:

\textbf{Definition.} A value \code{v : t} is called a \textit{binary search tree} if:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item It is \code{Empty}.
  \item It is \code{Node (L, x, R)}, where both \code{L} and \code{R} are binary
  search trees, and \code{x} is greater than or equal to every
  element in \code{L}, and less than or equal to every element in \code{R}.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{parts}
  \part[10]\hfill

  First, we will define a helper function:

  Implement a function \code{isBST'} which adheres to the following specification:

  \spec
    {isBST'}
    {(int -> bool) -> tree -> (bool -> 'a) -> 'a}
    {\code{p} is total}
    {\code{isBST' p T k} evaluates to \code{k true} if \code{T} is a binary
    search tree, and every element in \code{T} satisfies \code{p}. Otherwise,
    it evaluates to \code{k false}.}

  \begin{constraint}
    You must write this function in CPS, and with no helper functions.
  \end{constraint}

  \begin{codeblock}
    fun isBST' p Empty k =
  \end{codeblock}
  \begin{solutionorbox}[4em]
    \begin{codeblock}
      k true
    \end{codeblock}
  \end{solutionorbox}

  \begin{codeblock}
      | isBST' p (Node (L, x, R)) k =
  \end{codeblock}
  \begin{solutionorbox}[12em]
    \begin{codeblock}
      isBST' (fn y => y <= x andalso p y) L (fn is_bst_l =>
      isBST' (fn y => x <= y andalso p y) R (fn is_bst_r =>
      k (is_bst_l andalso is_bst_r andalso p x)))
    \end{codeblock}
  \end{solutionorbox}

  \newpage

  \part[2]\hfill

  Implement a function \code{isBST''}, using \code{isBST'}, with the following
  specification:

  \spec
    {isBST''}
    {tree -> (bool -> 'a) -> 'a}
    {\code{true}}
    {\code{isBST'' T k} evaluates to \code{k true} if \code{T} is a binary
    search tree. Otherwise, it evaluates to \code{k false}.}

  \begin{constraint}
    For full points, do not use a \code{fun} declaration.
    You may use precisely one lambda expression.
  \end{constraint}

  \begin{solutionorbox}[20em]
    \begin{codeblock}
      val isBST'' = isBST' (fn x => true)
    \end{codeblock}
  \end{solutionorbox}
\end{parts}

\newpage
\titledquestion{Fusing Transformations}

\textbf{Fusing Transformations}

We would like to apply geometric transformations to scalar values.
Each transformation is one of the following:

\begin{codeblock}
  datatype transform = Scale of int | Shift of int | Flip
\end{codeblock}

where \code{Scale k} multiplies a value by \code{k}, \code{Shift k} adds
\code{k} to it, and \code{Flip} negates it. A \code{transform list} is thus a
\textit{pipeline}: we apply its transformations in order.

Each transformation is really just an \textbf{affine map} --- a function of the
form \code{fn x => a * x + b} --- which we represent as the pair of its
coefficients \code{(a, b)}:

\begin{codeblock}
  type affine = int * int

  fun toAffine (Scale k) = (k, 0)
    | toAffine (Shift k) = (1, k)
    | toAffine Flip      = (~1, 0)
\end{codeblock}

A first attempt at applying a series of affine maps to a value is to map each transformation to a normal
function, and then apply them serially:

\begin{codeblock}
  fun toFn (tf : transform) : int -> int =
    let
      val (a, b) = toAffine tf
    in
      fn x => a * x + b
    end

  fun run (ts : transform list) : int -> int =
    let
      val fns = map toFn ts
    in
      fn input => foldl (fn (f, acc) => f acc) input fns
    end
\end{codeblock}

This works, but it's inefficient: the returned function does $n$
applications of the affine maps. We can do better---we can
exploit the property that any sequence of affine maps is itself a single
affine map.

\newpage

\begin{parts}
  \part[2]\hfill

    First, implement \code{combine}, which fuses two affine maps into one.
    \code{combine (g, f)} is the affine map equivalent to applying \code{f} first,
    then \code{g}.

    \textbf{Hint:} This is math-based. What is the result of applying
    $x \mapsto a_1 x + b_1$ and then $y \mapsto a_2 y + b_2$?

    \spec
      {combine}
      {affine * affine -> affine}
      {\code{true}}
      {\code{combine ((a2, b2), (a1, b1))} is the affine map equivalent to
      applying \code{(a1, b1)} and then \code{(a2, b2)}.}

    \begin{solutionorbox}[10em]
      \begin{codeblock}
        fun combine ((a2, b2), (a1, b1)) = (a2 * a1, a2 * b1 + b2)
      \end{codeblock}
    \end{solutionorbox}

  \part[4]\hfill

    Now implement \code{fuseAll}, which collapses a list of affine maps into the
    single affine map that applies all of them in order, head first.

    \spec
      {fuseAll}
      {affine list -> affine}
      {\code{true}}
      {\code{fuseAll ms} is the affine map equivalent to applying every map in
      \code{ms} to a coordinate, in order, head first.}

    \begin{constraint}
      Your solution must be \textit{point-free}: you may not use any
      \code{fun} declarations or \code{fn} expressions.
    \end{constraint}

    \begin{solutionorbox}[10em]
      \begin{codeblock}
        val fuseAll = foldl combine (1, 0)
      \end{codeblock}
    \end{solutionorbox}

  \newpage

  \part[4]\hfill

    Finally, implement \code{compile}, which stages a pipeline: it fuses the
    whole pipeline into one affine map \textit{once}, and returns a function that
    applies that single map to an input value.

    \spec
      {compile}
      {transform list -> (int -> int)}
      {\code{true}}
      {\code{compile ts} evaluates to a function that, given a coordinate
      \code{x}, applies the pipeline \code{ts} to \code{x} in order.}

    \begin{constraint}
      Your function must be properly staged: it should do the minimal number
      of actual multiplication and addition operations.
    \end{constraint}

    \begin{solutionorbox}[16em]
      \begin{codeblock}
        fun compile ts =
          let
            val (a, b) = fuseAll (map toAffine ts)
          in
            fn x => a * x + b
          end
      \end{codeblock}
    \end{solutionorbox}

\end{parts}

\newpage
\titledquestion{Cursed Predicates}

\textbf{Cursed Predicates}

An important idea is that the specific form of data
in a program does not matter, it only matters that producers and
consumers agree on a common \textit{contract}.

Standard predicate functions return a \code{bool}, which can be cased or
conditioned on later. Here, we discuss a cursed setup instead---instead of
returning a \code{bool}, a
\textbf{cursed predicate} signals its answer through \textit{control flow}: it
calls a continuation when it is satisfied, and raises an exception when it is
not. Assume the following have been declared:

\begin{codeblock}
  exception Drop
  type ('a, 'b) cursed_pred = 'a -> (unit -> 'b) -> 'b
\end{codeblock}

The contract for a value \code{cp : ('a, 'b) cursed\_pred}\footnote{We
need two type parameters because the type of the output is independent
of the type of the element being scrutinized.} is: for all values
\code{x} and \code{k : unit -> 'b}, evaluating \code{cp x k} calls \code{k ()} if
\code{x} satisfies the predicate, and raises \code{Drop} otherwise.

\noindent
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.49\textwidth}
  \centering
  \textbf{Regular predicate}
  \[
    \code{p x} \eeq
    \begin{cases}
      \code{true} & \text{if } \code{x} \text{ satisfies } \code{p}  \\
      \code{false} & \text{else}
    \end{cases}
  \]
\end{minipage}%
\hfill%
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.49\textwidth}
  \centering
  \textbf{Cursed predicate}
  \[
    \code{cp x k} \eeq
    \begin{cases}
      \code{k ()} & \text{if } \code{x} \text{ satisfies } \code{p} \\
      \code{raise Drop} & \text{else}
    \end{cases}
  \]
\end{minipage}

\begin{parts}
  \part[2]\hfill

    First, show that a cursed predicate carries no more information than an
    ordinary one, by implementing a function \code{curse} that converts a real
    predicate into a cursed one.

    \spec
      {curse}
      {('a -> bool) -> ('a, 'b) cursed_pred}
      {\code{p} is a total predicate}
      {\code{curse p} is a cursed predicate that fulfills its contract analogously
      to the regular predicate \code{p}.}

    \begin{solutionorbox}[10em]
      \begin{codeblock}
        fun curse p = fn x => fn k => if p x then k () else raise Drop
      \end{codeblock}
    \end{solutionorbox}

  \newpage

  \part[6]\hfill

    Now show that we can still consume the library, by reimplementing
    \code{List.filter} using a cursed predicate.

    Here, our cursed predicate is of type \code{('a, 'a list) cursed_pred}
    because \code{cursed_filter} has to return an \code{'a list}.

    \spec
      {cursed_filter}
      {('a, 'a list) cursed_pred -> 'a list -> 'a list}
      {\code{cp} is a cursed predicate}
      {\code{cursed_filter cp l} evaluates to the list of all elements of \code{l}
      which satisfy \code{cp}, in order.}

    \begin{constraint}
      You may not use \code{List.filter}, \code{List.foldr}, or \code{List.foldl}.
    \end{constraint}

    \begin{codeblock}
      fun cursed_filter cp [] =
    \end{codeblock}
    \begin{solutionorbox}[4em]
      \begin{codeblock}
        []
      \end{codeblock}
    \end{solutionorbox}

    \begin{codeblock}
        | cursed_filter cp (x::xs) =
    \end{codeblock}
    \begin{solutionorbox}[10em]
      \begin{codeblock}
        cp x (fn () => x :: cursed_filter cp xs)
        handle Drop => cursed_filter cp xs
      \end{codeblock}
    \end{solutionorbox}

\end{parts}

\newpage
\titledquestion{Robust Folding}

\textbf{Robust Folding}

We've seen the folding function, which lets us accumulate a result over
a list by applying a custom folding function.

Up until now we've assumed the folding function is total, but let's suppose
it is unreliable --- on some elements, it might \code{raise} an exception rather
than produce a result. Rather than let a single failure abort the entire fold, we
would like to \textit{skip} the offending element, continue folding, and
collect every exception that was raised along the way.

\spec
  {robust_foldl}
  {('a * 'b -> 'b) -> 'b -> 'a list -> 'b * exn list}
  {\code{true}}
  {\code{robust_foldl f acc l} folds \code{f} over \code{l} from left to right.
  For each element, if \code{f} raises an exception, the exception is recorded
  in a list of errors, and the element is skipped; otherwise, it is folded
  normally. It evaluates to a pair of the final accumulator and the list of all
  raised exceptions.}

For instance, if we have
\begin{codeblock}
    exception Stop
    fun f (122, y) = raise Stop
      | f (x, y) = x + y
\end{codeblock}
then \code{robust_foldl f 0 [4, 122, 5, 122]} $\eeq$ \code{(9, [Stop, Stop])}: the
successful elements \code{4} and \code{5} contribute to the accumulator, and
each failure is recorded in order.

\begin{constraint}
  You must use \code{foldl}. The folding function \code{f} may raise
  \textit{any} exception.
\end{constraint}

\begin{parts}
  \part[8]\hfill

\begin{solutionorbox}[14em]
  \begin{codeblock}
    fun robust_foldl f acc l =
      foldl
        (fn (x, (a, errs)) =>
          (f (x, a), errs)
          handle e => (a, e :: errs))
        (acc, [])
        l
  \end{codeblock}
\end{solutionorbox}
\end{parts}

\newpage

\titledquestion{Matching with Prefixes}

\textbf{Matching with Prefixes}

Consider the \code{regexp} datatype, as shown in lecture:

\begin{codeblock}
  datatype regexp =
      Zero
    | One
    | Char of char
    | Times of regexp * regexp
    | Plus of regexp * regexp
    | Star of regexp
\end{codeblock}

Regular expressions are cool and all, but one weakness of the \code{match}
function is that we don't know what the \code{char list} that was actually
matched \textit{was} -- we only know what comes after it!

Consider the following refinement of the \code{match} function:

\spec
  {matchWithPrefix}
  {regexp -> char list -> (char list * char list -> bool) -> bool}
  {\code{k} total}
  {\code{matchWithPrefix r cs k} evaluates to \code{true} iff
  \code{cs} $\eeq$ \code{p @ s}, where \code{p} $\in L(\code{r})$ and
  \code{k (p, s)} $\eeq$ \code{true}. Otherwise, it evaluates to \code{false}.
  }

Note the different specification! The key is simply that \code{k} takes in
\textit{both} \code{p} and \code{s}.

On the next page, we will implement some cases of \code{matchWithPrefix}:

\vspace{130pt}

THIS SPACE INTENTIONALLY LEFT BLANK

\newpage

\begin{parts}
  \part[12]\hfill

  Implement the \code{Char} and \code{Times} cases for the function
  \code{matchWithPrefix} here. The other cases have been provided for you.

  For your convenience, the \code{match} function has been provided on the next page.

  Hint: Draw a picture. For brevity, you can abbreviate \code{matchWithPrefix}.

  \begin{codeblock}
    fun matchWithPrefix
          (r : regexp)
          (cs : char list)
          (k : char list * char list -> bool) : bool =
      case r of
        Zero => false
      | One => k ([], cs)
  \end{codeblock}
  \vspace{-12pt}
  \begin{codeblock}[autogobble=false]
  | Char c =>
  \end{codeblock}
  \vspace{-24pt}
  \begin{solutionorbox}[13em]
    \begin{codeblock}
            case cs of
              [] => false
            | c'::cs' =>
                c = c' andalso k ([c], cs')
    \end{codeblock}
  \end{solutionorbox}
  \begin{codeblock}[autogobble=false]
  | Times (r1, r2) =>
  \end{codeblock}
  \vspace{-24pt}
  \begin{solutionorbox}[13em]
    \begin{codeblock}
            matchWithPrefix r1 cs (fn (pre, suff) =>
              matchWithPrefix r2 suff (fn (pre2, suff2) =>
                k (pre @ pre2, suff2)
              )
            )
    \end{codeblock}
  \end{solutionorbox}
  \begin{codeblock}
    (* FILL IN ABOVE *)
      | Plus (r1, r2) =>
          matchWithPrefix r1 cs k orelse matchWithPrefix r2 cs k
      | Star r =>
          (* this case is long. don't worry about it. *)
  \end{codeblock}
\end{parts}

\newpage

\textbf{Appendix: The \code{match} function}

\vspace{10pt}

\begin{codeblock}
  fun match (r : regexp)
            (cs : char list)
            (k : char list -> bool) : bool =
    case r of
      Zero => false
    | One => k cs
    | Char c => (case cs of
        [] => false
      | c' :: cs' => c = c' andalso k cs')
    | Plus (r1,r2) => match r1 cs k orelse match r2 cs k
    | Times (r1, r2) => match r1 cs (fn cs' => match r2 cs' k)
    | Star r =>
        k cs orelse
        match r cs (fn cs' =>
          cs' <> cs andalso match (Star r) cs' k
        )
\end{codeblock}




\end{questions}




